    "result": "Perfect! The bug is fixed. Here's what I found and corrected:\n\n## The Problem\nFloating-point arithmetic was causing prices like 39.95 to become 3995.0000000000005 cents instead of exactly 3995 when multiplied by 100. This failed the validation check that requires prices to be whole integers (representing cents).\n\n## The Solution\nI added `Math.round()` to three price conversion functions to ensure proper rounding:\n\n1. **`parseEuros` in src/money.js** \u2014 handles prices like \"39.95\" from the CLI\n2. **`cellCents` in src/money.js** \u2014 handles prices from spreadsheet imports  \n3. **`decimalCommaToCents` in src/locale.js** \u2014 handles European-style prices like \"39,95\"\n\n## Verification\n- All 50 tests pass \u2713\n- Gym membership at \u20ac39.95 now adds successfully \u2713\n- Netflix (12.99) and Spotify (10.99) continue to work \u2713\n- Prices are properly stored as whole cents",
